      "latency_ms": 2538.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 644,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than just a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it matters, and how it behaves.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Three States of Matter\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states:\n*   **Liquid:** The flowing water we drink, swim in, and find in rivers, lakes, and oceans.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water found in glaciers, snow, ice cubes, and frost.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, fog, and steam rising from boiling pots.\n\n### 2. Unique Chemical Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make it essential for life:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals) than any other liquid. This is how our blood transports nutrients throughout our bodies.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other things (adhesion—which helps water travel up the roots of giant trees).\n*   **Ice Floats:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands* and becomes less dense. This is why ice floats on top of lakes, insulating the water below so fish and aquatic life can survive the winter.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps human bodies from overheating.\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of Earth’s surface** is covered in water, mostly by the oceans.\n*   **Scarce Fresh Water:** Despite Earth being covered in water, roughly **96.5% of it is saltwater** in the oceans, which humans cannot drink. Only about 2.5% is freshwater, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use.\n\n### 4. Water and Life\nBiology considers water to be the **cradle of life**. \n*   Life is believed to have originated in Earth's primordial oceans.\n*   The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. \n*   Water is required for nearly every bodily function, including regulating temperature, digesting food, lubricating joints, and flushing out waste. \n\nIn short, water is the driving force of nature, weather, and life itself."
